<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Saint Padarn</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">6th century</span> · <a href="/centuries/6th-century">6th century</a> · Also known as Paternus of Llanbadarn</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Saint Padarn, in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin">Latin</a> Paternus, was a 6th-century Welsh saint, traditionally a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">bishop</a> and the founder of the monastery of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/llanbadarn-fawr">Llanbadarn Fawr</a> near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aberystwyth">Aberystwyth</a>. Llanbadarn became one of the leading churches of medieval <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wales">Wales</a> and the seat of an early bishopric. His later Latin Life links him with Saints <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/david">David</a> and Teilo, and several Welsh churches are dedicated to him.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/saint-padarn.jpg" alt="Saint Padarn" width="320" height="591" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Massalim · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Saint_Patern.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>The main source for Padarn is the Vita Sancti Paterni, perhaps an epitome of a longer work. It makes him Armorican by race, the son of Petran and Guean, who dedicated themselves to Christ before Petran left Letavia, modern Brittany, for Ireland. Canon G. H. Doble held that this Breton origin can no longer be maintained and favoured a Welsh one, while Bowen thought Padarn was later confused with Breton saints of the same name. Around 524, the Life says, he went to join his father in Ireland and travelled to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a> with his cousin Saint Cadvan.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>Padarn studied at Cor Tewdws, the school of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/illtud">Illtud</a>, and then founded the monastery of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/llanbadarn-fawr">Llanbadarn Fawr</a>, which became the seat of a new diocese with him as its first <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">bishop</a>. In the Life, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maelgwn-gwynedd">Maelgwn Gwynedd</a> tries to cheat the monastery of its property, is struck blind and sick, and is cured only when he begs forgiveness and grants the community land from the mouth of the river Rheidiol to the Clarach. Padarn also travels to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jerusalem">Jerusalem</a> with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saint-david">Saint David</a> and Saint Teilo to be ordained bishop by the patriarch, and returns with a crozier and a finely woven tunic.</p><p>Finally he goes back to Letavia, builds a monastery at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vannes">Vannes</a> and joins the six <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">bishops</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/armorica">Armorica</a> as the seventh, which is why he is counted among the seven founding saints of Brittany. He appears to be the same person as the first bishop of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bracara-augusta">Braga</a> and as Saint Paternus of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/avranches">Avranches</a> in Normandy.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Legacy</h2><p>In the Life's best-known episode a tyrant named Arthur tries to take Padarn's tunic, is swallowed by the earth up to his chin, and is released only when he begs forgiveness and takes the saint as his patron. The story is too late to be reliable and was probably meant to raise Padarn's standing; it makes his Life one of the saints' lives that mention Arthur independently of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/geoffrey-of-monmouth">Geoffrey of Monmouth</a>. His feast day is 16 April. Besides <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/llanbadarn-fawr">Llanbadarn Fawr</a>, churches are dedicated to him at Llanberis in Gwynedd and in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/radnorshire">Radnorshire</a>, and St Padarn's Institute at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/llandaff">Llandaff</a> trains ministers of the Church in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wales">Wales</a>.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Padarn” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The kingdom covered the coastal lowlands and hills of modern Ceredigion, a region of small farms and cattle-rearing, and its church centres included Llanbadarn Fawr, associated with <b>St Padarn</b>, which became one of the leading monasteries of Wales.</blockquote><a href="/state/kingdom-of-ceredigion">Kingdom of Ceredigion</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wales">Wales</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/united-kingdom">the United Kingdom</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vannes">Vannes</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/france">France</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=52.35,-3.63&amp;year=564" aria-label="Wales on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4468 357 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Saint Padarn"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M4918 627h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M4918 627h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=52.35,-3.63&amp;year=564">What was here in 564 CE? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
